Name the ops a model ran, so each can be optimized where it lives. The model is {{ model }},
run by {{ framework }}; its config is config.json here. ops.json lists the ops that took
{{ coverage }} of device time, keyed by op: the framework op, the op that owns it, the kernels
it launched with their device time, its argument names, shapes and dtypes, and its call site.
The kernel repositories on this machine are in {{ repos }}.

For each key, find where the op is implemented -- the registration of its namespace, the
kernel it launches, the source behind it -- by reading the framework and the repositories,
not by guessing from names. Then write names.json:

{"ops": {"<key>": {
   "route": "kernel" | "library" | "aten" | "unnamed",
       kernel:  a kernel in one of the repositories that Xe-Forge can rewrite;
       library: the op's time is a library primitive (e.g. oneDNN) -- what is tuned is the
                call, in the repository that makes it;
       aten:    implemented inside PyTorch, no repository to edit;
       unnamed: none of these could be established -- say why in "via".
   "name": the symbol to grep for in the repository (kernel function, or the function
           making the library call), "repo": the repository's directory name,
   "dsl": the language the kernel is written in ("sycl", "triton", "opencl", ...); for a
          library call, the language the call is made in, which its baseline reproduces,
   "via": one line: how you established it (file:line),
   "family": "gemm" | "attention" | "norm" | "activation" | "moe" | "rope" | "cache" |
             "sampling" | "copy" | "elementwise" | "other",
   "paged": true when an argument is a paged or allocated buffer (a KV cache, a block or
            page table) whose touched part depends on the context, not its shape,
   "gemm": only for a matrix multiply -- which argument is what, from the kernel's source:
     {"activation": "<arg>", "weight": "<arg>",
      "act_per_stored": <logical K elements per stored activation element, 1 unless packed>,
      "n_axis": <index of N in the weight's shape>,
      "weight_side": ["<arg>", ...]   every tensor read with the weight: weight, scales,
                                      zero points, bias -- not activation-side scales,
      "output": "<arg>" or null,
      "grouped": true for one call over several experts' matrices,
      "experts": "<integer argument holding the expert count>" or null}
 }},
 "model": {"topk": <experts each token is routed to, or null>,
           "experts": <routed experts in the model, or null>}}

Use the keys exactly as ops.json gives them. An op you cannot place is "unnamed" with the
reason, never a guess.
